# labor.fun log rotation — SAFETY NET against a runaway-log disk-fill.
#
# Rendered from this template by safe-deploy.sh (the ${DEPLOY_ROOT} placeholder
# is filled with this org's install root via envsubst) and installed to
# /etc/logrotate.d/<service> so a fresh deploy is protected by default. Runs
# under the system logrotate cron/timer (typically daily), but rotation is
# gated by SIZE, not time — see below.
#
# Background: the orchestrator, kb, and auto-deploy services write to
# ${DEPLOY_ROOT}/logs/*.log and *.error.log via systemd
# `StandardOutput/StandardError=append:...`. A hot-path WARN once flooded an
# error log to 682MB and filled the droplet's disk. Nothing rotated these.
#
# Design:
#   size 100M      Rotate on SIZE, not on a daily/weekly clock. A flood can
#                  fill the disk in hours — long before a time-based rotate
#                  would fire — so cap each file instead. logrotate rotates
#                  whenever its periodic run finds a file past 100M.
#   rotate 3       Keep 3 old copies (≈300M raw, far less compressed) then drop
#                  the oldest. Bounds worst-case disk use.
#   compress       gzip rotated copies to save space.
#   missingok      A log that doesn't exist yet (e.g. no error output) is fine.
#   notifempty     Don't rotate an empty file.
#   copytruncate   REQUIRED here. The services hold the log file descriptor
#                  open for the life of the process (systemd append:). The
#                  default rename+create (or `create`) rotation would leave the
#                  process still writing to the now-rotated inode: the "current"
#                  log would appear frozen while the rotated file kept growing
#                  unbounded — defeating rotation entirely and never freeing the
#                  disk. copytruncate instead copies the file, then truncates
#                  the original in place, so the held fd keeps writing to the
#                  same (now-empty) inode. The small race (a few log lines
#                  written between copy and truncate can be lost) is an
#                  acceptable trade for logs. Do NOT switch to create/rename
#                  unless the app is changed to reopen its log fds on SIGHUP.
#
# The glob deliberately matches *.log, which also covers *.error.log (and any
# future logs the framework writes into this dir).
${DEPLOY_ROOT}/logs/*.log {
    size 100M
    rotate 3
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
}
